import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,al as n}from"./chunks/framework.BKGVwAqU.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Projects and tasks","description":"How projects and task types work in VERA and why logging against the right one keeps your data accurate.","frontmatter":{"description":"How projects and task types work in VERA and why logging against the right one keeps your data accurate."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/projects.md","filePath":"guide/projects.md"}'),r={name:"guide/projects.md"};function s(i,e,c,l,h,d){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="projects-and-tasks" tabindex="-1">Projects and tasks <a class="header-anchor" href="#projects-and-tasks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Projects and tasks&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Every hour you log in VERA is attached to a project. Most are also linked to a task — the type of work you did. Understanding how these work makes logging faster and keeps your team&#39;s data accurate.</p><hr><h2 id="projects" tabindex="-1">Projects <a class="header-anchor" href="#projects" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Projects&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A project is a body of work — a client engagement, an internal initiative, a recurring retainer. Your organization&#39;s projects are created and configured by an owner or manager in the console.</p><p><strong>Projects that started before VERA.</strong> If a project&#39;s earlier work was never recorded here, an owner or admin can tick <strong>Work before VERA isn&#39;t recorded here</strong> under Edit project. VERA then doesn&#39;t judge that project&#39;s LER and doesn&#39;t grade it, because the hours behind its fee were never recorded, and it leaves its project manager&#39;s grade. Its revenue still counts in the P&amp;L and Billing.</p><p>When you log time, you choose the project from the <strong>project dropdown</strong> in the Log time modal. Only active projects you can log against appear there, so you pick from a real list rather than typing a name.</p><hr><h2 id="tasks" tabindex="-1">Tasks <a class="header-anchor" href="#tasks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tasks&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Tasks are categories of work within a project — Design, Development, Strategy, Admin, Client Management, and so on. Each project has a specific set of tasks enabled for it, and the <strong>Task dropdown</strong> only offers those.</p><p>A new workspace starts with five tasks already set up: Design, Development and Project Management are billable, Admin and Meeting are not, and each of Design, Development, Project Management and Admin counts toward the discipline of the same name. A new client project starts with Design, Development, Project Management and Meeting enabled, and a new internal project with Admin, Meeting and Project Management. Owners can change any of this in Settings → Tasks.</p><p>If your team has set a default task for your discipline, the modal pre-selects it for the project you chose — you can change it any time. Internal projects (admin, training, and similar) are task-optional, so you can leave the task blank there.</p><hr><h2 id="project-types" tabindex="-1">Project types <a class="header-anchor" href="#project-types" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Project types&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Your organization may run different types of projects. As a team member, the type mostly affects how your manager and owner see the financial picture — it doesn&#39;t change how you log time. The types are:</p><p><strong>Retainer</strong> — an ongoing engagement with a set number of hours and a monthly fee. Runs in periods (usually monthly).</p><p><strong>Time &amp; Materials (T&amp;M)</strong> — billed by the hour. Hours logged directly drive the invoice.</p><p><strong>Fixed Price</strong> — a set fee for a defined scope. Revenue is recognized as the work is completed.</p><p><strong>Agile / Sprint</strong> — work delivered in sprints, each with its own scope and value.</p><p><strong>Pro Bono</strong> — client work at no charge.</p><p><strong>Internal</strong> — overhead, training, admin, R&amp;D, and similar non-client work.</p><hr><h2 id="work-plans-allocations" tabindex="-1">Work plans (allocations) <a class="header-anchor" href="#work-plans-allocations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Work plans (allocations)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Your manager may give you a work plan on a project — an &quot;allocation&quot; of hours. Your time on any of the project&#39;s tasks counts toward it. It&#39;s how planned effort is tracked: allocated vs logged vs remaining.</p><p>Each allocation also shows where you&#39;ll land at your current pace. VERA divides the hours you&#39;ve recorded by the share of the project&#39;s time that has gone by: 6.4h recorded 10% of the way through a 64h allocation lands on 64h, on course. 27h recorded 30% of the way through lands at 90h, 41% over, and the allocation turns amber. Nothing turns amber until 20% of the project&#39;s time has passed, so a busy first week raises no alarm. An allocation turns red only once its hours are used up.</p><p>Work plans are planning tools, not gates. You can log time to any active project whether or not you have an allocation for it. Managers create and adjust allocations from the console; if you&#39;re regularly logging to projects that aren&#39;t part of your plan, let your manager know so they can keep your allocations current.</p><p>An allocation is a person and a number of hours, with nothing else to keep up. Its page lists the task links people recorded on their time (an Asana task, a Linear issue) with the hours on each, so you can see which pieces of work the hours went on.</p><p>The allocation&#39;s page also has a <strong>Time recorded</strong> card listing every time entry that counts toward it, newest first, with a total that matches the Logged figure.</p><p>The allocation&#39;s page also lists the tasks linked to the time recorded against it, with the hours for each, beside the links the PM planned. An entry with several links counts under each.</p>',29)])])}const u=t(r,[["render",s]]);export{g as __pageData,u as default};
